x86_64-linux  three differences, each already recorded: cxx1 writes the C1 forms of Held's and User's inline constructors where clang emits only what a use asks for - docs/CONFORMANCE.md, "An inline member function is a strong symbol" - and it names the static local `fromStatic.kept` where Itanium spells it _ZZ10fromStaticvE4kept, which the same file records under "A static local keeps its own name in the object file"
arm64-darwin  clang spells the zeroing of `new int[3]{}` `bzero` on Darwin where cxx1 calls `memset`, the same libc call under two names - and the same line agrees on the other two targets
x86_64-windows  the static local again, and newly visible rather than newly wrong: clang names it ?kept@?1??fromStatic@@YAHXZ@4UPlain@@A and cxx1 spells it its own way, which is the difference the x86_64-linux line above already records for the Itanium spelling. It surfaced when tools/mangled-names learned to read `.comm` and `.lcomm` - clang defines a zero-initialised internal object with those and the scrape saw neither, so this symbol had been invisible on both this target and Linux rather than agreed.
